<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="{{ str_replace('_', '-', app()->getLocale()) }}"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Knowledge Sharing · DiaCare</title><x-theme-script /><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.bunny.net"><link href="https://fonts.bunny.net/css?family=instrument-sans:400,500,600,700,800&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />@vite(['resources/css/app.css','resources/js/app.js'])</head><body class="landing-page"><header class="landing-nav"><a href="{{ route('welcome') }}" class="landing-brand"><span class="brand-mark brand-logo"><svg viewBox="0 0 36 36"><path d="M18 3.5 29 8v8.5c0 7.2-4.7 12.9-11 16-6.3-3.1-11-8.8-11-16V8l11-4.5Z"/><path d="m11.5 18 4 4 9-9"/></svg></span><span>DiaCare</span></a><nav><a href="{{ route('welcome') }}">Home</a><a href="{{ route('welcome') }}#about">About</a><a href="{{ route('assistant') }}">AI Chatbot</a><a class="active" href="{{ route('knowledge') }}">Knowledge Sharing</a><a href="{{ route('quiz') }}">Quiz</a><x-theme-toggle /><a class="landing-link" href="{{ route('login') }}">Login</a><a class="landing-cta" href="{{ route('register') }}">Sign up</a></nav></header><main class="public-content"><section class="public-hero"><span class="landing-kicker">Knowledge Sharing</span><h1>Diabetes information,<br><em>made easier to explore.</em></h1><p>A practical, beginner-friendly overview of diabetes, everyday records, food, activity, medication, and when to ask for help.</p></section><section class="knowledge-grid knowledge-grid-wide"><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill blue">The basics</span><h2>What is diabetes?</h2><p>Diabetes is a chronic condition involving how the body regulates blood glucose. Glucose is an important source of energy, and insulin helps move glucose from the blood into cells.</p><details><summary>Why does glucose matter?</summary><p>Too much or too little glucose can affect how a person feels and functions. A reading is a snapshot, while several readings and their context can help show patterns.</p></details></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill purple">Types</span><h2>Common types</h2><p><strong>Type 1</strong> is an autoimmune condition in which insulin-producing cells are damaged. <strong>Type 2</strong> involves insulin resistance and reduced insulin production. <strong>Gestational diabetes</strong> develops during pregnancy.</p><details><summary>Are all types managed the same way?</summary><p>No. Care plans differ. Your healthcare team can explain your diagnosis, targets, medication, and monitoring plan.</p></details></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill orange">Possible signs</span><h2>Symptoms to discuss</h2><p>Possible symptoms may include increased thirst, frequent urination, tiredness, blurred vision, or unexplained weight changes. Some people have few symptoms, so testing and professional advice matter.</p><details><summary>What should I do?</summary><p>Discuss persistent or concerning symptoms with a healthcare professional. Do not use a quiz or AI response as a diagnosis.</p></details></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill green">Monitoring</span><h2>Make records useful</h2><ul><li>Record the value, time, and context.</li><li>Note whether it was before or after a meal.</li><li>Record activity, illness, stress, or unusual events.</li><li>Look at patterns instead of reacting to one number.</li></ul></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill blue">Food</span><h2>Meals and carbohydrates</h2><p>Carbohydrates are broken down into glucose. Portion size, fiber, protein, fat, and the combination of foods can influence digestion and energy. A meal photo can support memory, but AI nutrition values are estimates.</p><details><summary>Helpful meal habits</summary><p>Follow the meal plan provided for you, pay attention to portions, include foods that fit your care plan, and discuss questions with a dietitian or clinician when available.</p></details></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill green">Movement</span><h2>Activity and exercise</h2><p>Activity uses energy and may affect glucose. Tracking the type and duration can help you notice connections with meals and readings.</p><details><summary>Keep it personal</summary><p>Choose activity that is appropriate for your health and follow professional guidance, especially if you have complications or use glucose-lowering medication.</p></details></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill purple">Medication</span><h2>Use medicines safely</h2><p>Take medicines according to the instructions from your healthcare professional. DiaCare can remind you of a schedule but does not prescribe, change, or confirm doses.</p><details><summary>If a dose is missed</summary><p>Follow the medicine label or the instructions from your healthcare professional. If unsure, contact a pharmacist or clinician rather than guessing or doubling a dose.</p></details></article><article class="knowledge-card"><span class="topic-pill orange">Do / avoid</span><h2>Everyday safety</h2><ul><li>Do keep appointments and ask questions.</li><li>Do keep your records private.</li><li>Avoid changing treatment without advice.</li><li>Avoid ignoring repeated or concerning readings.</li></ul></article></section><section class="knowledge-note"><strong>When to seek help</strong><p>If you have severe symptoms, confusion, fainting, breathing difficulty, severe dehydration, or another urgent concern, seek appropriate urgent medical help. Personal targets and treatment decisions should come from your healthcare team.</p></section></main><footer class="landing-footer"><div class="landing-brand"><span class="brand-mark brand-logo"><svg viewBox="0 0 36 36"><path d="M18 3.5 29 8v8.5c0 7.2-4.7 12.9-11 16-6.3-3.1-11-8.8-11-16V8l11-4.5Z"/><path d="m11.5 18 4 4 9-9"/></svg></span><span>DiaCare</span></div><a href="{{ route('welcome') }}">Home</a><span>© {{ date('Y') }} DiaCare</span></footer></body></html>
